2. TRAINING DATA PROVENANCE  --  READ BEFORE ANY COMMERCIAL USE
===============================================================================

No dataset is redistributed here; `data/` is gitignored and every corpus is
re-fetched from its source. But the licences below constrain what the trained
CHECKPOINTS may be used for, which is a separate question from the code.

  corpus                     terms (verify on the source)   used by
  -------------------------  -----------------------------  ---------------------
  Samanantar (ai4bharat)     CC BY-NC 4.0  NON-COMMERCIAL   ckpt_translate
  IIT Bombay en-hi (cfilt)   research / non-commercial      ckpt_translate
  codeparrot-clean           scraped GitHub; MIXED, incl.   ckpt_coder
                             GPL and AGPL source
  Wikimedia dumps (hi, en)   CC BY-SA  share-alike          every Hindi/English model
  C4 (allenai)               ODC-BY, over Common Crawl      ckpt_multi36k, ckpt_coder
  fineweb-edu                ODC-BY, over Common Crawl      ckpt_coder
  code_exercises (jinaai)    CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 NON-COMMERCIAL, ckpt_coder, ckpt_coder_sft
                             SHARE-ALIKE; generated by
                             ChatGPT 3.5 (see dataset card)
  OpenCodeInstruct (nvidia)  CC BY 4.0                      ckpt_coder_sft
  glaive-code-assistant      Apache 2.0                     ckpt_coder_sft
  FLORES-200                 CC BY-SA                       evaluation only
  HumanEval (openai)         MIT                            evaluation only
  MBPP (google-research)     CC BY 4.0                      evaluation only

  Release licences chosen from this table (docs/PUBLISHING.md section 1):
  the coder checkpoints CC BY-NC-SA 4.0, the translation checkpoint
  CC BY-NC 4.0, the Hindi/English base and Q&A checkpoints CC BY-SA 4.0.

  The three sharpest points:

  * `ckpt_coder` and `ckpt_coder_sft` learned from code_exercises, which is
    CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 and was generated with ChatGPT 3.5. That makes the coder
    weights non-commercial and share-alike, and a downstream model trained
    on their outputs may inherit the same terms.


  * `ckpt_translate` was trained on Samanantar and the IIT Bombay corpus,
    both of which carry non-commercial terms. It is the best-performing model
    in this project and it is the one NOT to ship commercially without
    resolving that.

  * `ckpt_coder` learned from codeparrot-clean, which is de-duplicated GitHub
    Python and therefore contains GPL- and AGPL-licensed source. Whether that
    propagates to model weights is unsettled.

  Whether training on copyrighted material creates any claim over the
  resulting weights is genuinely unsettled law and differs by jurisdiction.
  Nothing here is legal advice. For research, teaching, portfolio or internal
  use -- which is what this project is for -- none of it is a problem.

